ng women the Western the Islamic  which one do you consider better if you  assume I do that the Western value  system regarding women is better then we  are consider confronted with the next  step which is do you preserve or allow  these communities to preserve their ways  regarding women or do you refuse to  accept that and I think professor  somewhat Huntington would say on  American soil I don't think he would  propose changing them elsewhere but an  American soil on Western so I think he  would say we we should not practice  relativism there we should protect the  rights of all individuals regardless of  their heritage on American so that  entails a policy that protects the  victims of Islamic culture female  Islamic culture and the punishment of  the perpetrators but also a sustained  education in Muslim men and women to  give up their attitudes toward women and  their practices and their habits and to  adopt new ones where does the Universal  Declaration of Human Rights fit into  this because that provides a standard  for all men and women and the protection  of Human Rights  isn't that and shouldn't that be a  factor in all societies not just one it  is a factor for all societies but it is  an ideal in some places that ideal has  been reached  I think the United States is one of them  I don't think it's perfect and perfectly  round but I think most of what is in the  declaration of Universal Declaration of  Human Rights is actually implemented  in the West it is not in the rest of the  world one can say we are going to  campaign as Westerners outside of the  West for these universal rights and that  campaigning has delivered some results  some countries have signed and ratified  the Universal Declaration of Human  Rights even though they have not  implemented it you can keep pushing for  that what I saw and this is the product  of infidel and Nomad is even in the  United States in the United Kingdom in  Holland in France places where the  Universal Declaration of Human Rights as  far as it regards women has been adopted  and implemented that Muslim women are  excluded from that excluded from the  protection of the law there are Muslim  lobby organizations in Canada in the  u.s. in the UK lobbying for parts of  Sharia to be accepted describe what  Sharia law is I know here in the United  States it much has been written about it  but I'm sure our audience would be  interested in hearing your definition of  Sharia law Sharia law is Islamic law or  law derived from Islam Islam means  submission to the will of Allah was  founded by Muhammad bin Abdullah he  considers himself a prophet and he's  regarded by his followers as a prophet  initially in Mecca he had only preached  an attitude toward humanity and toward  one another of hospitality and charity  etc once in Medina when he fled from  Mecca he went to Ethiopia then called  Abyssinia landed in Medina and once in  Medina  his following became strong enough to  form a militia or militias and from  being a peace-loving peace preaching  prophet he turned into a warrior a  conqueror and at that time when his  following became so large almost as  large as a society at that time he said  that he had laws revealed to them that  must govern that society  and that set of laws that were revealed  to him and their father evolution is  termed Sharia or Islamic law now you  have to imagine those laws in this in  the context of the 7th century and as  they evolved the 8th 9th 10th centuries  the laws governing women and men at that  time were considered to be incredibly  progressive for instance in the context  of that Adam desert culture which was  very patriarchal men were allowed to  have an unlimited number of concubines  he reduced that to four men could leave  women as well he decided that he should  now have a dowry he introduced the law  of banning infanticide in that context  little children girls if they were  considered to be too many were buried  alive  he banned that was very very progressive  at that time but if you take Sharia law  from that time 7th century regarding  women and you apply it now in the 21st  century when countries like the United  States are governed by the Universal  Declaration of Human Rights and women  are equal to men and you disinherit  women they inherit half of what their  brother gets their testimony is worth  half of that of a man they get a so  called dowry that is determined by a  guardian and that is not really what  they want or at least does not serve  their interests that when raped they  have to produce they they have to  testify against the man who raped them  but their testimony is worth only half  all of these things that they have to  have a guardian all of these things that  in the 7th century seemed very  progressive in the 21st century are  really very very reactionary and  detrimental to women and that is my  criticism of sharia let's stop doing  everything as we used to do and look  into the past and trying to revive the  7th century let's look forward and to  Western society to Americans let's not  exclude women from the general law that  applies to all of us on  because they were born into Islam and  only because the perpetrators justify  their actions in the name of religion or  culture now when your book came out and  just recently the New York Times Sunday  magazine had you featured and there were  a series of questions asked in that in  the magazine and it the title of it was  feminist and it was casting you as a  feminist do you view yourself as a  feminist I view myself as a feminist in  the classical sense of the word when  feminists fought for the heights of  women  but these were Heights that were a  matter of life and death and they were a  matter of justice I have encountered  feminists who have a different set of  priorities feminists who feel that we  should all organize ourselves in order  to move in relationships between men and  women whether they are married or not  for 