n engaged in and also trying to  advance as having Christians and  speaking and sitting down with Muslims  and having if you will almost cross  religious discussions and opportunities  to benefit from the experiences develop  that a bit talk about that about your  ideas in that regard that you have  identified in the book and that you are  actually acting on upon well I feel like  in the West if you assume that someone  Huntington's thesis of a clash of  civilizations is correct I think I feel  that in the West we have been cornered  into an attitude of defense we are on  the defensive continues you're always  saying we are we are not Islamophobes we  are not discriminating we lie  q on the one hand on the other hand we  are using military means and spying  methods to deal with jihad and with the  violence against the West against women  etc what I would like us to do is to go  back to the realm of ideas assume that  Muslim individuals are endowed with  reason that if engaged in dialogue and I  mean improper dialogue where you don't  shy away and move around and beat about  the bush in the areas where there is a  real a real conflict of values but  freely address them that there is a  possibility of changing people's minds  just last night I was in Philadelphia  and it was on the heckard a young Muslim  woman stands up with a headscarf and she  challenges me she's from Afghanistan and  she challenges me she says I am born in  Afghanistan and the men in my family  well good to me they sent me to school I  am now in University in the United  States and it's because of the men in my  family I am I dare you to tell me what  what is it about the Koran that you  didn't like and I gave it I told how  well it's chapter 4 verse 34 the verse  that says beats your wife it is chapter  2 verse 222 that gives man permission to  rape their wives in in marriage women  from Afghanistan have protested against  this this was reported I read it in the  New York Times but this was reported in  the West this young woman was sitting  there jotting down those verses she's  going to look them up she's going to  link them I'm not saying that I've  changed her mind or that I've made her  into something else that she is not but  I was so inspired and encouraged by her  curiosity by wanting to know what it was  that I didn't want and I see most Muslim  people as such as that young woman  wanting to know they are a group of them  very elite very privileged living in the  United States I encounter them in  campuses that have this PostScript  narratives that they come with who are  not ready to listen but I just there to  grab the mic and you know do their thing  which is to defend the image of Islam  but they're not speaking for Islam  they're not speaking for Muslims they're  not speaking for this young woman who  wants to know who wants to learn for  whom learning is a grace it's a great  deal and I am sure that if Christians  were to do the same if feminists were to  do the same if Enlightenment thinkers  were to do the same that many many  Muslim minds but are now trapped in this  radical you know born-again Islam this  jihadist form of Islam would change  their minds and adopt new ideas are open  to something else other than following  the example of mohammed are you trying  to put together formal dialogues and  discussions toward that end  you're clearly speaking speaking to  different audiences and targeting some  here and there but in a systematic way  are you thinking about the organization  of these kinds of dialogues maybe with  some of the same people not just once  but several times is that something  that's on your agenda it is on my agenda  but again I believe in putting as a  priority really the most severe forms of  human rights violations I've started the  AHA Foundation which is a short form for  ayaan Hirsi Ali but also the  Enlightenment aha and at the I have  foundation with a number of American  women who said we don't just want to  talk we want to do something and I  pointed out to my experience in the  Netherlands and in Europe where for a  long time these practices of female  genital mutilation and forced marriages  honor killings child brides or going on  in society was looking the other way and  I said I suspect that something like  that might be going on in the United  States and with that foundation first of  all we want to start by informing the  American population that it is here  researching how many you know we don't  have data exactly to how many women as  this happen  and then educating and influencing the  American leadership to prevent these  things from happening and then from  there perhaps the young woman I just  described individuals like that will  emerge and will you know take the  debates themselves to a different level  there was another woman in that  Philadelphia library and he is I think  where dialogue pays off who was wearing  a headscarf  it's a practicing Muslim and said I do  exactly what you do but I only do it  undercover and I and I have you must've  asked her well why undercover I of  course even though I know it because a  lot of the readers of infidel women and  they writes to me and they write to me  about the consequences of what they face  there are they risk being shunned being  vilified character assassination being  beaten and sometimes even being killed  so the price that you pay for this  transition is very very very high and  some of us want to pay that price in our  own different way but we are there we  are there and I think that right now  the loudest voices that we hear others  people who are saying there's nothing  wrong with Islam it has nothing to do  with Islam or those people who are  actively winning hearts and minds or the  muslim community for the jihadi project  let me ask you about your foundation are  you looking at specifically these areas  that you identified in the United States  if I understood you correctly or you're  trying to educate Americans about these  acts against women and these human  rights violations against women  worldwide  oh no I believe in starting small I also  believe in charity begins at home  we are looking at these practices in the  United States and educating Americans in  the fifty states to look at their Muslim  communities and if they see these  practices to recognize it first as  something different and not the general  domestic violence and to do something  about it sometimes perhaps to adjust  legislation sometimes to take girls in  from the streets because sometimes when  you save a guy  from misogyny in her own family's house  she ends up in the streets and gets into  worse trouble and I want American  families to know that there are young  women often very bright and often very  keen to learn who whom they could adopt  and could help and become American and  live the American dream he's trafficking  and one of the issues that's on the  agenda or related to these communities  it is not the agenda of the Hopf  foundation but we work closely with the  Somali mom foundation who's really only  focused on combating trafficking  she's a Cambodian woman Somali mom who  is herself a victim of trafficking and  there are other women's rights  organizations that deal with that but if  we see anything that has to do with  trafficking and I'm sure there are many  Muslim women who are victims of  trafficking we we pass it on to the  organizations that we work together with  what's your recommendation to us policy  makers in terms of the violations  against women women's rights against  also the other aspect of your book in  terms of integration and how one can  help in the assimilation of those coming  to the United States and their  interrelationships here but what what  are your recommendations and how are you  trying to get your views out to policy  makers in Washington what I would like  to say to policy makers in Washington is  where the integration or the  assimilation