versation with my mother  is it's like a one-way traffic my mother  tries also to do the same thing prevents  me from in her understanding prevents me  from going to hell my half-sister did  exactly the same thing tries to convince  me to come back to Islam I have another  cousin who is much more on my side but  insists that she's still a Muslim and  doesn't really want to give up on the  religion so I have I mean I relate the  stories of  there is just something that you can  never take away when you grow up are  siblings is that familiarity and that  loyalty I'm looking after my brother son  and it's just that you know basic human  relationship that is I think much older  than religion absolutely let me ask you  in the United States many say that when  there are those who come here or of  course a a a country of immigrants and  many have come here with big dreams what  is your big dream what is it that you  hope for myself for yourself and and for  others both well my big hope for Muslims  in general and Muslims in the West and  Muslims in America is for them to shed  the political aspect and the social  aspect of Islam I can live with their  religious dimension I mean if people  want to pray that is fine but by doing  so they don't harm others but to shed  any inspiration they get from Islam to  do harm to themselves or others the big  dream for myself is to lead a life in  freedom and in safety and where I can  spend part of my life  contributing to what I think is the  improvement of humanity would you say  that you're already living that dream  for yourself I'm live at this time I'm  living that dream I'm living it within  the parameters of the security that I  have but I feel that I am living it and  I am really very very grateful to the  American Enterprise Institute that  brought me here and the many American  friends that I've already made who make  me feel every single day that I meet  them that this is a great nation and I  know why it's great and that d